Day 72 - 132 Pattern

Problem Statement:
Given an array of n integers nums, a 132 pattern is a subsequence nums[i], nums[j], nums[k] such that i < j < k and nums[i] < nums[k] < nums[j]. Return true if a 132 pattern exists, otherwise return false.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    bool find132pattern(vector<int>& nums) {
        stack<int> st;
        int n = nums.size();

        int second = INT_MIN;

        for(int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {

            if(nums[i] < second) {
                return true;
            }

            while(!st.empty() && nums[i] > st.top()) {
                second = st.top();
                st.pop();
            }

            st.push(nums[i]);
        }

        return false;
    }
};





Time Complexity:
O(n), because every element is pushed into and popped from the stack at most once.

Space Complexity:
O(n), for the stack.

Approach:
We traverse the array from right to left and use a stack.

st → stores possible nums[k] values.
second → stores the best possible nums[k] value for a 132 pattern.

For a valid pattern:

nums[i] < nums[k] < nums[j]

When nums[i] > st.top(), we pop elements from the stack and update second. This means we have found a possible nums[j] that is greater than nums[k].


